HUMAN = "No, you don't. Rounding errors happen, and if there's no change in the actual tax there's no reason to amend. If all the income was properly reported and the tax was properly calculated - no-one cares if it was rounded up or down on one of the lines. Note for the next time though: Not sure about New York, but Federal taxes are generally rounded to the nearest dollar on each line of the form. So don't calculate cents, just round to the nearest dollar, and be consistent on all of your tax forms. Technically, it is perfectly legal to report cents as well (and people used to when the forms were still filled by hand with pen), but all the automatic tax filing software rounds, so just do that."

AI = "In the UK, it is common for car insurance policies to exclude coverage for commuting to and from work. This means that if you are involved in a car accident while driving to or from work, your insurance company may not cover the damages or injuries resulting from the accident.However, this exclusion typically only applies to your regular commute to and from work. If you are driving for a reason other than your regular commute, such as running errands or going on a road trip, your insurance should provide coverage as normal.It is important to carefully review the terms of your car insurance policy to understand exactly what is and is not covered. If you have any questions about your policy, you should contact your insurance company or an insurance broker for more information."
